Let's first rigorously derive the form of $\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}$ by Lagrange Multiplier.
Since the goal is to minimize $\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i = 1}^n(X_i - x)^2 + \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i = 1}^n(Y_i - y)^2$ subject to $x^2 + y^2 = 1$, the Lagrangian is
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{L}(x, y, \lambda) = \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i = 1}^n(X_i - x)^2 + \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i = 1}^n(Y_i - y)^2 + \lambda(x^2 + y^2 - 1). 
\end{align*}
The stationarity condition $\nabla_{x, y, \lambda}\mathcal{L} = \boldsymbol{0}$ then requires a solution $(x_0, y_0, \lambda_0)$ to satisfy
\begin{align*}
&amp; -n\bar{X} + nx_0 + 2\lambda_0 x_0 = 0, \tag{1}\label{1} \\
&amp; -n\bar{Y} + ny_0 + 2\lambda_0 y_0 = 0, \tag{2}\label{2} \\
&amp; x_0^2 + y_0^2 = 1.  \tag{3}\label{3}
\end{align*}
Substituting $x_0 = \frac{n\bar{X}}{n + 2\lambda_0}$ (obtained by $\eqref{1}$) and $y_0 = \frac{n\bar{Y}}{n + 2\lambda_0}$ (obtained by $\eqref{2}$) into $\eqref{3}$ yields
\begin{align*}
n^2\bar{X}^2 + n^2\bar{Y}^2 = (n + 2\lambda_0)^2, 
\end{align*}
i.e., $n + 2\lambda_0 = n\sqrt{\bar{X}^2 + \bar{Y}^2} = n\|\boldsymbol{\bar{Z}}\|$, whence
\begin{align*}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}} = \begin{bmatrix} x_0 \\ y_0 \end{bmatrix} 
= \begin{bmatrix} \frac{n\bar{X}}{n\|\boldsymbol{\bar{Z}}\|} \\ 
\frac{n\bar{Y}}{n\|\boldsymbol{\bar{Z}}\|} \end{bmatrix} =  
\frac{\boldsymbol{\bar{Z}}}{\|\boldsymbol{\bar{Z}}\|}.
\end{align*}
The asymptotic normality of $\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}$ can be established by the Multivariate Delta Method. To this end, rewrite $\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}$ as
\begin{align*}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}} = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{\bar{X}}{\sqrt{\bar{X}^2 + \bar{Y}^2}} \\ \frac{\bar{Y}}{\sqrt{\bar{X}^2 + \bar{Y}^2}} \end{bmatrix} 
= \begin{bmatrix} h_1(\bar{X}, \bar{Y}) \\ h_2(\bar{X}, \bar{Y}) \end{bmatrix}
=: \boldsymbol{h}(\bar{X}, \bar{Y}), \tag{4}\label{4}
\end{align*}
where $h_1(x, y) = \frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}}$, $h_2(x, y) = \frac{y}{\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}}$. The gradient of $\boldsymbol{h}$ can be found as
\begin{align*}
\nabla\boldsymbol{h}(x, y) = 
\begin{bmatrix}
\frac{\partial h_1}{\partial x} &amp; \frac{\partial h_1}{\partial y} \\
\frac{\partial h_2}{\partial x} &amp; \frac{\partial h_2}{\partial y} 
\end{bmatrix} = 
\begin{bmatrix}
\frac{y^2}{(x^2 + y^2)^{3/2}} &amp; -\frac{xy}{(x^2 + y^2)^{3/2}}  \\
-\frac{xy}{(x^2 + y^2)^{3/2}}  &amp; \frac{x^2}{(x^2 + y^2)^{3/2}} 
\end{bmatrix}. \tag{5}\label{5}
\end{align*}
On the other hand, it follows by assumption that
\begin{align*}
\sqrt{n}\left(\begin{bmatrix} \bar{X} \\ \bar{Y} \end{bmatrix} - 
\begin{bmatrix} \mu_1 \\ \mu_2 \end{bmatrix}\right) \Rightarrow N(\boldsymbol{0}, I_{(2)}). \tag{6}\label{6}
\end{align*}
(In fact, the weak convergence symbol &quot;$\Rightarrow$&quot; in $\eqref{6}$ can be replaced with an exact &quot;$\sim$&quot; because of the normality assumption). It then follows by $\eqref{4}, \eqref{5}, \eqref{6}$ and the multivariate Delta method that (note that the constraint $\|\boldsymbol{\mu}\| = 1$ ensures the denominator in $\eqref{5}$ is non-zero when $x$ and $y$ are substituted by $\mu_1$ and $\mu_2$, and $\nabla\boldsymbol{h}(\mu_1, \mu_2)$ reduces to
$\begin{bmatrix} \mu_2^2 &amp; -\mu_1\mu_2 \\ -\mu_1\mu_2 &amp; \mu_1^2 \end{bmatrix}$):
\begin{align*}
&amp; \sqrt{n}\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}} - \begin{bmatrix} \mu_1 \\ \mu_2 \end{bmatrix} \right) = 
\sqrt{n}\left(\begin{bmatrix} h_1(\bar{X}, \bar{Y}) \\
h_2(\bar{X}, \bar{Y}) \end{bmatrix} - \begin{bmatrix} h_1(\mu_1, \mu_2) \\ h_2(\mu_1, \mu_2) \end{bmatrix} \right) \\ 
&amp; \Rightarrow N_2\left(\boldsymbol{0}, \begin{bmatrix} \mu_2^2 &amp; -\mu_1\mu_2 \\ -\mu_1\mu_2 &amp; \mu_1^2 \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix} \mu_2^2 &amp; -\mu_1\mu_2 \\ -\mu_1\mu_2 &amp; \mu_1^2 \end{bmatrix}\right) \\
&amp;= N_2\left(\boldsymbol{0}, 
\begin{bmatrix} \mu_2^4 + \mu_1^2\mu_2^2 &amp; -\mu_1\mu_2^3 - \mu_2\mu_1^3 \\
 -\mu_1\mu_2^3 - \mu_2\mu_1^3 &amp; \mu_1^4 + \mu_1^2\mu_2^2
\end{bmatrix}\right) \\
&amp;= N_2\left(\boldsymbol{0}, 
\begin{bmatrix} \mu_2^2 &amp; -\mu_1\mu_2 \\
 -\mu_1\mu_2 &amp; \mu_1^2
\end{bmatrix}\right). \tag{7}\label{7}
\end{align*}
That is, the asymptotic covariance matrix of $\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}$ is
\begin{align*}
\begin{bmatrix} \mu_2^2 &amp; -\mu_1\mu_2 \\
 -\mu_1\mu_2 &amp; \mu_1^2
\end{bmatrix}.
\end{align*}
Note this covariance matrix is degenerated (as its determinant is $0$), which meets the intuition that the squared sum of $\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}$ is always $1$.
Technically speaking, because the gradient matrix $\begin{bmatrix} \mu_2^2 &amp; -\mu_1\mu_2 \\
 -\mu_1\mu_2 &amp; \mu_1^2
\end{bmatrix}$ is not positive definite, while it's OK to present the asymptotic relationship as in $\eqref{7}$ (provided degenerated normal distributions are still normal distributions), it is better to reformulate it as (cf. Theorem 3.1 in Asymptotic Statistics by A. W. Van der Vaart)
\begin{align*}
\sqrt{n}\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}} - \begin{bmatrix} \mu_1 \\ \mu_2 \end{bmatrix} \right) \Rightarrow 
\begin{bmatrix}
\mu_2^2Z_1 - \mu_1\mu_2Z_2 \\
-\mu_1\mu_2Z_1 + \mu_1^2Z_2
\end{bmatrix},
\end{align*}
where $Z_1, Z_2 \text{ i.i.d. } \sim N(0, 1)$. This permits us to obtain the asymptotic variance of $\operatorname{Var}(\hat{\mu}_i)$ (which equals to $1 - \mu_i^2$) and the asymptotic covariance of $\operatorname{Cov}(\hat{\mu}_1, \hat{\mu}_2)$ (which equals to $-\mu_1\mu_2$) from a different angle (note that sequences such as $\{h_1(\bar{X}, \bar{Y})\}$ are uniformly integrable so $E[h_1(\bar{X}, \bar{Y})] \to E[\mu_2^2Z_1 - \mu_1\mu_2Z_2] = 0$, etc).

