answer_text:
There are a variety of issues here:

as @Sointu points out in comments, Version probably isn't feasible as a random effect because it only has two levels. You may be confusing nuisance variables (variables not of direct scientific interest) with random effects; they are not always the same (e.g. see discussion here), and in this case you should probably make Version a fixed effect. If you include Difficulty*Version (rather than Difficulty+Version) then you will also be accounting for the possibility of an interaction between order and difficulty.
I'm guessing that your experimental design has 10 'easy' passages and 10 'hard' passages. If so, you shouldn't try to fit random slopes for the Passage random effect, as these effects should be unidentifiable (each passage is either &quot;easy&quot; or &quot;hard&quot;, so you can't estimate the variation in the effect of difficulty across passages).
You might want to consider log-transforming your reaction times or using a Gamma response (typically with a log link), as these values often have skewed conditional distributions (take a look at the model diagnostics with the DHARMa package or performance::check_model())
I agree with the commenter that it's hard to guess exactly what the reviewer means by &quot;complete model results&quot;.

library(lmerTest)
