For example, if I specify a z-value of 3, then I would look at both sides and know its position in the distribution (99.73%).
Would this change if I have a left or right skewed distribution? Would I have to choose a lower threshold for the positive or negative calculation of the z-value to fall into the actual range of outliers? Is my thinking wrong?
By the term outlier, I mean an observation that deviates from the concept of normality. In this case, I would mean something that lies at the edge of the distribution.

Below is the z-score method I mentioned (to find outliers above a certain threshold):
def outliers_z_score(ys):
    threshold = 3;
    mean_y = np.mean(ys);
    stdev_y = np.std(ys);
    z_scores = [(y - mean_y) / stdev_y for y in ys];
    outliers_ind = np.where(np.abs(z_scores) &gt; threshold);
    return [z_scores, outliers_ind];

