dev=(null.dev - ext.residual.deviance)/null.dev is the critical line for understanding the situation. This can be written as:
$$
A=\dfrac{
B - C
}{
B
}
$$
Your situation is when $A&lt;0$, so $\dfrac{
B - C
}{
B
}&lt;0
$. A few steps of algebra shows that this is equivalent, assuming $B&gt; 0$ (safe assumption in this case), to $C&gt;B$.
$B$ is the null deviance, while $C$ is the model deviance. Thus, the model deviance is higher than the null deviance. That is, the model does a worse job of explaining, in some sense, the data than the null model does.
It may be disappointing, sure, that all of your hard work in fitting a model still can't beat a simple benchmark in the null model. However, this is still useful information. Before you put your model in production, you expose it for being rather poor.
